[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Django Signals: Pre/Post Hooks, Custom Signals, and Anti-Patterns"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Django signals allow decoupled applications to get notified when certain actions occur elsewhere in the framework. While powerful, they must be used judiciously to avoid hidden control flows and debugging nightmares."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Built-in Signals: Pre and Post Save/Delete"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The most commonly used signals in Django are model-level lifecycle hooks: pre_save, post_save, pre_delete, and post_delete. These execute before or after a model instance is saved or deleted."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"from django.db.models.signals import post_save, pre_save
from django.dispatch import receiver
from .models import UserProfile

@receiver(pre_save, sender=UserProfile)
def user_profile_pre_save(sender, instance, **kwargs):
    if not instance.slug:
        instance.slug = instance.generate_unique_slug()

@receiver(post_save, sender=UserProfile)
def user_profile_post_save(sender, instance, created, **kwargs):
    if created:
        # Perform actions only on creation
        print(f"Profile created for {instance.user.username}")"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating Custom Signals"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can define your own signals by instantiating the Signal class. This is useful for decoupling domain events, such as when a user completes a subscription or an order ships."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"from django.dispatch import Signal

# Define a custom signal with providing_args (informational in modern Django)
order_shipped = Signal()

# In your business logic:
# order_shipped.send(sender=Order, order=self, tracking_number="12345")"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Connecting and Receiving Custom Signals"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To listen for your custom signal, use the @receiver decorator just like you would for built-in signals."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"@receiver(order_shipped)
def handle_order_shipped(sender, **kwargs):
    order = kwargs.get('order')
    tracking = kwargs.get('tracking_number')
    print(f"Notifying customer about order {order.id} with tracking {tracking}")"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Signal Anti-Patterns and Best Practices"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Anti-pattern: Using signals for core business logic. Signals are invisible and make stack traces hard to follow. Keep core CRUD logic in model save() methods or service layers."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Anti-pattern: Circular imports. Always import your signal receivers inside the ready() method of your app configuration class (AppConfig)."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Best Practice: Keep signal receivers idempotent and lightweight. Offload heavy tasks like sending emails or generating PDFs to background workers like Celery."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"# apps.py configuration example
from django.apps import AppConfig

class AccountsConfig(AppConfig):
    default_auto_field = 'django.db.models.BigAutoField'
    name = 'accounts'

    def ready(self):
        import accounts.signals  # Explicitly load signal handlers"}]
